{%- comment -%}
  GOD SQUAD — Collection page
  Phase 10. The catalogue browse surface. Every View all button on the home
  page, every collection link in the merchant's menu and every
  /collections/<handle> URL lands here.

  Sources of truth:
  - PHASE-1-WEBSITE-AUDIT.md SHOP-09, which lists templates/collection.json
    among the storefront routes that have no template and therefore serve
    Shopify's error page. Phase 8 recorded the same gap from the other end:
    snippets/cart-empty-state.liquid points its one button at the home page
    rather than at routes.all_products_collection_url precisely because no
    collection template existed. This file is what closes it.
  - PHASE-2-DESIGN-SYSTEM.md section 8 (the container model), 9.3 (the grid),
    12 (form controls), 13 (the card), 10 (buttons).
  - sections/featured-collection.liquid, whose grid, sizes arithmetic and card
    call this file reuses rather than reinvents.

  SHOPIFY IS THE SOURCE OF TRUTH.
  The title, the description, the banner image, the product set, the sort
  order and the number of results are all read from the collection object.
  There is no product data, no price string, no ranking and no copy about the
  collection anywhere in this file.

  IT IS THE SAME GRID, NOT A SECOND ONE.
  The tiles are {% render 'product-card' %} inside .product-grid, which is the
  markup featured-collection emits and the CSS component-product-card.css
  already styles — including the mobile gap and track floor that Phase 6
  measured. The sizes arithmetic below is the same calculation Phase 9
  corrected there, with the copy-column branches removed: this band has no copy
  rail, so the grid is the full content row at every tier.

  THE SORT CONTROL DELIBERATELY DROPS THE PAGE NUMBER.
  A GET form does not append to its action's query string. The browser
  discards whatever query the action URL carries and rebuilds it from the
  form's own fields, so submitting this form sends sort_by and nothing else,
  the page parameter included. That is not a side effect being tolerated, it is
  the behaviour this surface wants, and it is why there is no hidden page
  input here. Page 7 of "Price, low to high" holds different products from
  page 7 of "Newest", so carrying the number across drops the customer into
  the middle of a set they never scrolled; and where one order has seven pages
  and another six, it drops them on a page that does not exist. A new sort
  starts the result set again.

  Nothing else on this URL needs carrying. Faceted filtering is out of Phase 10
  scope and this section renders no filter control, so sort_by is the only
  parameter the surface owns. A later phase that adds filters must put their
  values in this form as hidden inputs, or the same discard that drops the page
  number will drop the filters.

  ONE SMALL SCRIPT, AND EVERYTHING WORKS WITHOUT IT.
  The rest of this section is static markup. The script below does two things
  and only two: it submits the form when the select changes, and it then hides
  the submit button that made the form usable without it. The order matters —
  the button is hidden by the script that replaced it, not by a CSS rule keyed
  off the document's js class, because that class is set in the head and would
  hide the button even on a page where this script never ran.

  WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE.
  No faceted filtering, which the Phase 10 brief excludes. No infinite scroll
  or load-more, both of which need script to work at all and leave a customer
  with no way to link to what they are looking at. No collection subheading,
  promotional band, badge, count-down or "X people are viewing this" — those
  are business facts nobody has supplied. No sub-collection navigation: the
  menu is the merchant's, and this section does not invent a second one.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign cols_d = section.settings.columns_desktop
  assign cols_t = section.settings.columns_tablet
  assign cols_m = section.settings.columns_mobile

  assign surface_class = 'surface-light'
  assign cta_variant = 'button--primary'
  if section.settings.surface == 'dark'
    assign surface_class = 'surface-dark'
    comment
      Gold is 11.01:1 on ink and 1.55:1 on cream, so the accent variant exists
      on the dark surface only — Phase 2 section 10.1. The empty state's single
      call to action takes it there and falls back to the primary variant on
      cream, which is the same derivation our-story.liquid makes.
    endcomment
    assign cta_variant = 'button--accent'
  endif

  comment
    The banner image is the collection's own, set in Shopify admin. There is no
    image_picker here on purpose: an image chosen in this section would be the
    same picture on every collection that uses this template, which is every
    collection in the shop.
  endcomment
  assign banner_image = collection.image
  assign show_banner = false
  if section.settings.show_image and banner_image != blank
    assign show_banner = true
  endif

  assign show_description = false
  if section.settings.show_description and collection.description != blank
    assign show_description = true
  endif

  comment
    Sorting renders only where Shopify offers options for this collection. A
    manually ordered collection still returns the full list, but the guard
    keeps the toolbar from rendering an empty select if it ever does not.
  endcomment
  assign show_sorting = false
  if section.settings.show_sorting and collection.sort_options.size > 0
    assign show_sorting = true
  endif
  assign current_sort = collection.sort_by | default: collection.default_sort_by

  comment
    Filters exist only once a merchant has created them in Shopify admin, under
    Apps > Search & Discovery > Filters. Until then collection.filters is empty
    and nothing below renders — no empty Filter button, no empty drawer.

    A group with a single value is dropped by snippets/facets.liquid, so a
    collection that happens to hold one vendor does not get a control whose only
    effect is to filter nothing out. has_filters therefore counts the groups
    that snippet would actually render, not the ones Shopify returned.
  endcomment
  assign has_filters = false
  if section.settings.show_filters
    for f in collection.filters
      if f.type == 'price_range'
        assign has_filters = true
      elsif f.values.size > 1
        assign has_filters = true
      endif
    endfor
  endif

  assign active_filter_count = 0
  for f in collection.filters
    assign active_filter_count = active_filter_count | plus: f.active_values.size
    if f.type == 'price_range'
      if f.min_value.value != blank or f.max_value.value != blank
        assign active_filter_count = active_filter_count | plus: 1
      endif
    endif
  endfor

  assign sort_form_id = 'CollectionSort-' | append: section.id
  assign sort_id = 'CollectionSortBy-' | append: section.id

  comment
    The form posts back to the path the customer is already on, not to
    collection.url. collection.url is the collection's canonical address and
    drops the tag segment of a legacy tag URL — /collections/all/faith comes
    back as /collections/all — so sorting from a tag view would silently throw
    the tag away along with the page number. The layout's own title block
    handles current_tags, so tag views are a state this theme already expects
    to be in. request.path carries the segment and carries no query string,
    which is exactly the pair of properties this form wants.
  endcomment
  assign sort_action = request.path
  if sort_action == blank
    assign sort_action = collection.url
  endif

  assign empty_url = section.settings.empty_link
  if empty_url == blank
    comment
      The home page, not routes.all_products_collection_url. This template now
      makes /collections/all servable, so that route is no longer a dead end —
      but an empty collection reached FROM /collections/all would send the
      customer back to the page they are standing on. The merchant can point
      the button at a collection once there is one worth pointing at.
    endcomment
    assign empty_url = routes.root_url
  endif

  comment
    The sizes attribute.

    The section knows the grid, so the section computes it and the card takes
    it as a parameter. This is Phase 6's calculation as Phase 9 corrected it,
    with the copy-column branches dropped, because two things still make it
    more than a division.

    First, the column count is a CEILING. .product-grid floors every track at
    --product-col-min 272px, so where a viewport cannot carry the requested
    count the grid drops a column and the survivors stretch. Dividing by the
    requested count would declare a slot narrower than the tile actually
    painted and the browser would fetch a candidate it has to upscale. Phase 6
    measured that: at 1024 the three-column grid painted two 292px tracks
    against a declared 196px. Between a tier's minimum width and the width at
    which the requested count first fits, the rendered count is exactly one
    lower, and that threshold is computable here — so each tier emits two
    clauses and both are exact across their range.

    Second, the row available to the grid is the viewport less the gutters:
    24, 32 and 48 per side on the Phase 2 ladder. This band has no copy rail,
    so there is nothing else to subtract.
  endcomment

  comment
    The sizes attribute is derived by snippets/grid-sizes.liquid, shared with
    the other grid that uses this exact arithmetic.

    It used to be ninety duplicated lines here. Phase 10 found that the desktop
    clause fired below its own breakpoint, booked it as a blocker, and fixed it
    in ONE of the copies; the other carried the bug for two more phases. Fixing
    an instance is not fixing a rule.
  endcomment
  assign chrome_d = 96
  capture sizes_attr
    render 'grid-sizes', cols_d: cols_d, cols_t: cols_t, cols_m: cols_m, chrome_d: chrome_d
  endcapture

  assign classes = 'main-collection '
  assign classes = classes | append: surface_class
  if show_banner
    assign classes = classes | append: ' main-collection--has-banner'
  endif
-%}

{%- comment -%}
  A main-* section is bound to templates/collection.json and cannot be added
  anywhere else, so it always renders and the stylesheet link sits at the top
  of the file rather than inside a configuration guard.

  component-product-card.css is linked here and not from the layout, the same
  way featured-collection links it: the shipped templates render one of the two
  sections or the other, never both, so no page gets two link tags for the one
  URL. A merchant who drops a collection row onto this template in the editor
  would; if that turns out to be a configuration anyone actually ships, the
  file moves to layout/theme.liquid under the same rule that promoted the
  button system in Phase 6 and the cart line in Phase 8.
{%- endcomment -%}
{{ 'component-product-card.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
{{ 'section-main-collection.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
{%- comment -%}
  Loaded with the section rather than from the layout, and only when there is
  something to filter: a store that has configured no filters pays nothing for
  the feature. facets.js is deferred like every other script here — the panel
  works without it.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if has_filters -%}
  {{ 'component-facets.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
  <script src="{{ 'facets.js' | asset_url }}" defer="defer"></script>
{%- endif -%}

{%- comment -%}
  The column counts are merchant settings, so they arrive as scoped custom
  properties rather than as inline style on the grid: an inline declaration
  would outrank every media query and pin the mobile count to all three tiers.
{%- endcomment -%}
{% style %}
  #shopify-section-{{ section.id }} .product-grid { --product-cols: {{ cols_m }}; }
  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    #shopify-section-{{ section.id }} .product-grid { --product-cols: {{ cols_t }}; }
  }
  @media (min-width: 1024px) {
    #shopify-section-{{ section.id }} .product-grid { --product-cols: {{ cols_d }}; }
  }
{% endstyle %}

<div
  class="{{ classes }}"
  data-collection-page
  data-collection-section="{{ section.id }}"
  {{ section.shopify_attributes }}
>
  {%- if show_banner -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      Full-bleed, so it is a child of the root rather than of __inner — the
      Phase 2 section 8 container model, the same place our-story puts its
      photograph.

      No aspect ratio is imposed. The token file has no banner ratio, and
      cropping a merchant's collection image to one invented here would hide
      part of a picture nobody on this project has seen. The image keeps its
      own proportions and the stylesheet caps its height instead, so a tall
      upload cannot push the whole grid below the fold.

      Alt text is the collection image's own, set in Shopify admin. Where it is
      empty the image is announced as decorative, which is the correct default
      for a mood photograph above a heading that already names the collection.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="main-collection__banner">
      {{
        banner_image
        | image_url: width: 2400
        | image_tag:
          class: 'main-collection__banner-image',
          widths: '480, 640, 900, 1200, 1500, 1800, 2100, 2400',
          sizes: '100vw',
          loading: 'eager',
          decoding: 'async',
          alt: banner_image.alt
      }}
    </div>
  {%- endif -%}

  <div class="main-collection__inner container">
    <div class="main-collection__header">
      {%- comment -%}
        The page's only level-1 heading, and it is the collection's own title.
        Unlike a content section's heading this is not a clearable setting, so
        the card titles below are a fixed level 2 rather than a derived level.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <h1 class="main-collection__title">{{ collection.title }}</h1>

      {%- if show_description -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          Shopify stores a collection description as HTML, so it is output
          unfiltered and the stylesheet handles the elements it may contain.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <div class="main-collection__description rte">{{ collection.description }}</div>
      {%- endif -%}
    </div>

    {%- paginate collection.products by section.settings.products_per_page -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        paginate.items, not collection.products.size.

        Inside a paginate block collection.products is the PAGE SLICE, so an
        out-of-range ?page= — which Shopify answers with a 200 and an empty
        slice, not a 404 — made a collection with products announce "This
        collection is empty." The same reasoning is already written out above
        for the product count, which correctly reads paginate.items: it is the
        size of the set actually being paginated.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- if paginate.items > 0 -%}
        {%- if has_filters -%}
          {% render 'facets', results: collection, form_id: sort_form_id, id_prefix: sort_id %}
        {%- elsif section.settings.show_filters and request.design_mode -%}
          {%- comment -%}
            Editor only. Filters are an admin setup step, not a theme one, and a
            merchant who switched this on and saw nothing would reasonably file
            it as a bug. Says nothing on the live storefront.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <p class="main-collection__notice">
            {{ 'sections.main_collection.no_filters' | t }}
          </p>
        {%- endif -%}

        {%- if show_sorting or section.settings.show_product_count or has_filters -%}
          <div class="main-collection__toolbar">
            {%- if has_filters -%}
              {%- comment -%}
                Server-rendered hidden, and unhidden only by assets/facets.js.
                Not keyed off the layout's `js` class: that class is set in the
                head before any script has run, so it would hide the panel on a
                page where facets.js never executed — the same reasoning the
                sort control records. With no script the panel is simply in
                flow above the grid and this button never appears.
              {%- endcomment -%}
              <button
                type="button"
                class="button button--secondary main-collection__filter-toggle"
                data-facets-toggle
                aria-expanded="false"
                hidden
              >
                {{ 'collection.filters.open' | t }}
                {%- if active_filter_count > 0 -%}
                  <span class="main-collection__filter-count">{{ active_filter_count }}</span>
                {%- endif -%}
              </button>
            {%- endif -%}
            {%- if section.settings.show_product_count -%}
              {%- comment -%}
                paginate.items rather than collection.products_count: it is the
                size of the set actually being paginated, so it stays correct
                if a later phase narrows that set.
              {%- endcomment -%}
              <p class="main-collection__count">
                {{ 'collection.product_count' | t: count: paginate.items }}
              </p>
            {%- endif -%}

            {%- comment -%}
              ONE form for the whole surface. It is rendered whenever there is a
              sort control OR a filter control, because every facet checkbox
              binds to it by the HTML form attribute rather than by sitting
              inside it — so submitting from the toolbar or from the drawer's
              Apply carries the sort and every checked filter together.

              Before Phase 13 this form existed only for sorting, and its own
              comment above recorded that it "sends sort_by and nothing else".
              That was correct while nothing else was in the URL. The moment
              filters arrived it would have discarded every one of them.

              No page parameter is rendered, so any submission returns to page 1
              — which is also what Shopify's own url_to_add does.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            {%- if show_sorting or has_filters -%}
              <form
                method="get"
                action="{{ sort_action }}"
                id="{{ sort_form_id }}"
                class="main-collection__sort"
                data-collection-sort
              >
                {%- if show_sorting -%}
                <label class="main-collection__sort-label" for="{{ sort_id }}">
                  {{ 'collection.sort_label' | t }}
                </label>
                <select
                  class="main-collection__sort-select"
                  id="{{ sort_id }}"
                  name="sort_by"
                  data-collection-sort-select
                >
                  {%- for option in collection.sort_options -%}
                    <option
                      value="{{ option.value }}"
                      {% if option.value == current_sort %}selected{% endif %}
                    >
                      {{ option.name }}
                    </option>
                  {%- endfor -%}
                </select>

                {%- endif -%}

                {%- comment -%}
                  The control that makes the form work with no script at all.
                  It carries no name attribute, so it contributes nothing to the
                  query string when it is the thing that submitted the form, and
                  nothing named "submit" shadows the form's own submit method.
                {%- endcomment -%}
                <button
                  type="submit"
                  class="button button--secondary main-collection__sort-submit"
                  data-collection-sort-submit
                >
                  {{ 'collection.sort_submit' | t }}
                </button>
              </form>
            {%- endif -%}
          </div>
        {%- endif -%}

        <ul class="product-grid" role="list">
          {%- for product in collection.products -%}
            {%- liquid
              comment
                The first image on the page is the one worth fetching eagerly.
                Where a banner renders it is that image, and every card stays
                lazy; where none does, the first card is the topmost image on
                the page and takes the eager load instead.
              endcomment
              assign card_eager = false
              if forloop.first and show_banner == false
                assign card_eager = true
              endif
            -%}
            <li class="product-grid__item">
              {% render 'product-card',
                product: product,
                heading_level: 2,
                sizes: sizes_attr,
                eager: card_eager
              %}
            </li>
          {%- endfor -%}
        </ul>

        {%- if paginate.pages > 1 -%}
          {%- comment -%}
            A real navigation landmark with a name, because a page can carry
            more than one nav and "Pagination" is what tells them apart.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          {% render 'pagination', paginate: paginate %}
        {%- endif -%}
      {%- else -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          A collection with nothing in it is a real state a customer can reach
          from the menu, so it gets a real answer and a way out — the pattern
          snippets/cart-empty-state.liquid established for the other empty
          surface in this theme. That snippet is not rendered here: its three
          strings are about a cart, and a shared empty-state component with a
          strings parameter is worth building only once a third surface needs
          one.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          Two different nothings. A collection with no products at all is a
          merchandising state the customer cannot act on; a collection whose
          ACTIVE FILTERS match nothing is one they can fix in a click, and
          telling them the collection is empty would be false. The filtered case
          therefore gets its own words and a Clear all rather than a link away.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- if active_filter_count > 0 -%}
        <div class="main-collection__empty">
          <p class="main-collection__empty-title">{{ 'collection.filters.none_match.title' | t }}</p>
          <p class="main-collection__empty-body">{{ 'collection.filters.none_match.body' | t }}</p>
          <a class="button {{ cta_variant }}" href="{{ collection.url }}">
            {{ 'collection.filters.clear_all' | t }}
            {% render 'icon-arrow' %}
          </a>
        </div>
        {%- else -%}
        <div class="main-collection__empty">
          <p class="main-collection__empty-title">{{ 'collection.empty.title' | t }}</p>
          <p class="main-collection__empty-body">{{ 'collection.empty.body' | t }}</p>
          <a class="button {{ cta_variant }}" href="{{ empty_url }}">
            {{ 'collection.empty.link' | t }}
            {% render 'icon-arrow' %}
          </a>
        </div>
        {%- endif -%}
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- endpaginate -%}
  </div>
</div>

{%- comment -%}
  THE SORT CONTROL SUBMITS ON A BUTTON, NOT ON CHANGE.

  An earlier version of this section submitted the form from the select's
  change event and then hid the submit button. That is the pattern most themes
  ship, and it is a WCAG 2.2 SC 3.2.2 On Input failure at Level A: changing the
  setting of a control automatically caused a change of context — a full page
  navigation — with no advance warning. It is worst for the users least able to
  absorb it, because a keyboard user arrowing through the options triggers a
  navigation on every option they pass.

  Keeping the button is the whole fix. The form is a plain GET form, so it now
  behaves identically with and without JavaScript, and this section carries no
  script at all — which also means it no longer emits a second root-level
  element beside its own wrapper.

  ZERO JavaScript.
{%- endcomment -%}

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Collection page",
  "tag": "section",
  "class": "main-collection-section",
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Banner"
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "show_image",
      "label": "Show the collection image",
      "default": true,
      "info": "The image comes from the collection itself, set in Shopify admin, so every collection keeps its own. Collections with no image simply start at the title."
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "show_description",
      "label": "Show the collection description",
      "default": true,
      "info": "Also written on the collection in Shopify admin. Nothing is written in the theme."
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Browsing"
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "show_product_count",
      "label": "Show how many products are in the collection",
      "default": true
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "show_filters",
      "label": "Show filters",
      "default": true,
      "info": "Filters are created in Shopify admin under Apps › Search & Discovery › Filters. Until at least one exists, nothing is shown here whatever this is set to."
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "show_sorting",
      "label": "Show the sort control",
      "default": true,
      "info": "The orders offered are Shopify's own and follow what the collection allows. Changing the sort starts again at the first page, because the same page number holds different products in a different order."
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "products_per_page",
      "label": "Products per page",
      "min": 8,
      "max": 48,
      "step": 4,
      "default": 24,
      "info": "Anything beyond this count moves to a second page with numbered links. There is no load-more button: it needs JavaScript to work at all, and it leaves a customer with no way to link to what they are looking at."
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Layout"
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "surface",
      "label": "Colour scheme",
      "info": "Cream is the approved treatment for browsing, and is what the home page's product rows use.",
      "options": [
        {
          "value": "light",
          "label": "Cream"
        },
        {
          "value": "dark",
          "label": "Ink"
        }
      ],
      "default": "light"
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Grid"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "These are maximums. A tile never drops below a readable width, so if a viewport cannot carry the count you choose, the grid drops a column rather than squeezing one."
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "columns_desktop",
      "label": "Columns, desktop",
      "min": 2,
      "max": 4,
      "step": 1,
      "default": 4
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "columns_tablet",
      "label": "Columns, tablet",
      "min": 1,
      "max": 3,
      "step": 1,
      "default": 2
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "columns_mobile",
      "label": "Columns, mobile",
      "min": 1,
      "max": 3,
      "step": 1,
      "default": 2
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "What a tile shows — price, original price, colourway swatches — is set once for the whole catalogue and is not a per-page choice. A shop that prices its products in one place and not another is a bug rather than a decision."
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Empty collection"
    },
    {
      "type": "url",
      "id": "empty_link",
      "label": "Where the empty-collection button goes",
      "info": "Leave empty to use the home page. Point it at a collection that has products in it once there is one."
    }
  ],
  "enabled_on": {
    "templates": [
      "collection"
    ]
  }
}
{% endschema %}
